course: geography
lesson: 58
title: Deep time and the story of life
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: The Earth is about 4.54 billion years old, a span so huge that geologists call it deep time. If all of Earth's history were squeezed into one day, humans would appear only in the last few seconds before midnight.
explain: How do we know? Some atoms are radioactive and slowly change into other atoms at a steady rate, like sand falling through an hourglass. By measuring how much of each is in a rock, scientists can work out its age.
explain: For billions of years life was only microbes. Tiny sunlight-using microbes slowly filled the air with oxygen from about 2.4 billion years ago; about 540 million years ago animals with shells, eyes and legs burst onto the scene in the Cambrian explosion.
explain: Plants reached land, four-legged animals crawled out, and great disasters struck: about 252 million years ago about 90% of sea species died out, and about 66 million years ago an asteroid about 10 km wide hit Mexico and ended the non-bird dinosaurs, leaving room for mammals.
fact: The Earth is about 4.54 billion years old.
fact: Radioactive atoms decay into other atoms at a fixed rate, so measuring both tells the age of a rock; the time it takes for half of them to change is called a half-life.
fact: Earth's history is divided into eons, eras and periods, such as the Jurassic, about 200 to 145 million years ago, and the Cretaceous.
fact: The first life, simple microbes, appeared at least 3.5 billion years ago, possibly earlier.
fact: Tiny microbes that used sunlight slowly filled the air with oxygen, starting about 2.4 billion years ago.
fact: About 540 million years ago, many animal groups with shells, eyes and legs appeared fairly quickly, in the Cambrian explosion.
fact: Plants moved onto land about 470 million years ago.
fact: The first four-legged animals crawled out of the water about 375 million years ago.
fact: The biggest mass extinction, about 252 million years ago, wiped out about 90% of sea species.
fact: About 66 million years ago an asteroid about 10 km wide hit Mexico and ended the age of non-bird dinosaurs.
fact: Mammals had lived as small animals beside the dinosaurs; after the dinosaurs, mammals spread into the empty places in nature and grew into many forms, from whales to elephants.
fact: Geologists call the vast span of Earth's history deep time, far longer than human history; humans appeared only about 300,000 years ago.
exercise: How old is the Earth? | About 4.54 billion years | About 6,000 years | About 4.54 million years | About 454 billion years
exercise: How do scientists find the age of a rock? | By measuring radioactive atoms that decay at a fixed rate | By counting the rings inside the rock, one ring for every year | By weighing the rock on a scale | By asking how old the hill looks
exercise: How is Earth's history divided? | Into eons, eras and periods | Into weeks, months and years | Into kingdoms and great empires | Into centuries and nothing else
exercise: Which of these is a geological period? | The Jurassic | The Renaissance | The Victorian age | The Space age
exercise: What was the first life on Earth? | Simple microbes | Dinosaurs and reptiles | Ferns and tall trees | Fish in the deep sea
exercise: When did the first life appear? | At least 3.5 billion years ago | About 66 million years ago, with the dinosaurs | About 10,000 years ago | About 540 million years ago
exercise: What filled the air with oxygen? | Tiny microbes that used sunlight | Volcanoes blowing it out of the ground | Dinosaurs breathing it out into the air | The asteroid that hit Mexico long ago
exercise: When did oxygen start filling the air? | About 2.4 billion years ago | About 2.4 million years ago | About 24,000 years ago | About 240 years ago
exercise: What was the Cambrian explosion? | Many animal groups with shells, eyes and legs appearing fairly quickly | A huge volcano that blew up a whole continent and covered it in ash | The asteroid that ended the dinosaurs | The first oxygen in the air
exercise: When did plants move onto land? | About 470 million years ago | About 4.7 billion years ago | About 47,000 years ago | About 4,700 years ago
exercise: When did the first four-legged animals crawl out of the water? | About 375 million years ago | About 3.75 billion years ago | About 37,500 years ago | About 3,750 years ago
exercise: How bad was the biggest mass extinction, about 252 million years ago? | About 90% of sea species died out | About 1% of sea species died out | Every living thing on Earth died | Only the dinosaurs died out, nothing else
exercise: What ended the age of non-bird dinosaurs? | An asteroid about 10 km wide hitting Mexico | A great flood over all the land | Early humans hunting them with spears and fire | Mammals eating all their eggs
exercise: What happened after the non-bird dinosaurs died out? | Mammals spread into many forms, from whales to elephants | Life on Earth stopped for millions of years | Dinosaurs came back a million years later, smaller and faster | Fish took over the land
exercise: What is deep time? | The vast span of Earth's history | The time at the bottom of the sea | The time it takes to dig a mine | The hours you sleep at night
test: A cartoon shows cavemen riding dinosaurs. What is wrong? | Non-bird dinosaurs died about 66 million years before humans | Cavemen were too small to ride them, but they did live side by side | Dinosaurs were too slow | Nothing, they lived together
test: If Earth's 4.54 billion years were one day, which came first? | Microbes, early in the day | Dinosaurs, at breakfast time | Humans, at noon | Plants on land, just after midnight
test: A rock has half its radioactive atoms left after one half-life. After a second half-life, how much is left? | A quarter | Nothing at all | Half, still | Three quarters
test: Why could no animals like us live on Earth 3 billion years ago? | There was almost no oxygen in the air yet | It was too dark, because there was no Sun yet | The sea was made of lava | There was no water on Earth at all
test: "dinosaurs are totally extinct, none left" — Zoe. Is she quite right? | Not quite: birds are living dinosaurs; only the non-bird ones died out | Yes, every dinosaur relative is gone | No, T. rex still lives in deep jungles where nobody has looked properly | No, dinosaurs turned into fish
test: What would probably have happened if the asteroid had missed Earth 66 million years ago? | Non-bird dinosaurs might have stayed dominant and mammals stayed smaller | Humans would have appeared 66 million years earlier for sure | The Earth would have stopped spinning | Plants would never have reached land
test: Which order is right, oldest first? | First microbes, oxygen in the air, Cambrian explosion, plants on land | Plants on land, first microbes, oxygen, Cambrian explosion | Cambrian explosion, first microbes, plants on land, oxygen | Oxygen, plants on land, first microbes, Cambrian explosion
test: A teacher stretches a 4.5 km rope, 1 metre for every million years. About how long is the part since the dinosaurs died? | About 66 metres | About 66 centimetres | About 2 km of rope | About 4 km, almost all of it
test: my mate goes "earth's like a few thousand years old innit". What do rocks show? | About 4.54 billion years, from radioactive dating | About 4,500 years, from old books | About 45 million years, from fossils of the first humans | No one can know anything about it
test: Why was the biggest mass extinction worse than the one that ended the dinosaurs? | It wiped out about 90% of sea species | It was caused by a bigger asteroid that hit the Moon | It happened only last century | It killed only plants
test: Why did whales and elephants appear only after the dinosaurs? | Mammals spread into empty roles once the dinosaurs were gone | Whales hatched from dinosaurs' eggs | Elephants lived in space before that | Mammals did not exist at all before then, not even small ones
test: In a game, the Jurassic comes after humans built cities. What is true? | The Jurassic was about 200-145 million years ago, long before humans | The Jurassic is the period we live in now | The Jurassic was 2,000 years ago | The Jurassic is a future period that starts after the last humans have gone
exam: Ascertain the age of the Earth as established by radiometric dating. | Approximately four thousand five hundred and forty million years | Approximately six thousand years | Approximately four million five hundred thousand years | Approximately forty-five thousand million years
exam: Identify the principle upon which the radiometric dating of rocks depends. | Radioactive atoms decay into other atoms at a constant rate | The colour of rock darkens at a constant and measurable rate | Rocks increase in mass at a constant rate | Fossils within rocks multiply at a constant rate
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Cambrian explosion? | The comparatively rapid appearance of numerous animal groups possessing shells, eyes and limbs | The volcanic eruption which extinguished the dinosaurs | The first appearance of oxygen within the atmosphere | The impact of an asteroid upon Mexico
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the termination of the age of non-avian dinosaurs. | It followed the impact of an asteroid approximately ten kilometres in diameter in Mexico | It followed the hunting of dinosaurs by early humans | It followed a global flood of the continents | It followed the appearance of oxygen in the atmosphere
